      "headline": "The Munger Approach: Invert, Wait, and Never Be Foolish",
      "keyInsight": "The world worships brilliant moves, but lasting success comes from avoiding stupid mistakes. Charlie Munger noticed a funny paradox in human nature: trying hard to be brilliant makes you sloppy.\n\nInstead of asking how to become rich, ask how to go bankrupt, and then avoid those traps. Instead of hunting for fifty average opportunities every year, sit on your hands and wait for one no-brainer.\n\nPatience looks lazy to amateurs, but it is the ultimate weapon of master investors. When you combine simple rules from biology, physics, and history, you spot hidden risks before anyone else even smells smoke.",
      "content": "Think of a master carpenter who carries only a hammer. To him, every loose screw, leaking pipe, and cracked window looks like a nail. If he tries to fix a plumbing leak by hammering it, the whole house floods.\n\nMost investors do the exact same thing. A stock trader looks only at price charts, while an accountant looks only at tax loopholes. Munger built a full toolbox instead, borrowing big ideas from physics, psychology, and engineering.\n\nHis secret weapon was a mental trick called inversion. Instead of asking, 'How do we sail this ship safely to London?' he asked, 'What would sink this ship tomorrow?' Once you identify icebergs, bad crew members, and leaky hulls, avoiding them is straightforward. In business, avoiding catastrophic errors does ninety percent of the heavy lifting.\n\nYou do not need to leap over seven-foot hurdles; just step over one-foot fences.\n\nMost people fail because their fingers itch to trade every morning. They confuse frantic motion with real progress. Munger compared smart investing to waiting for a fat pitch right over the center of the plate.\n\nYou can let dozens of risky balls fly past without swinging. Then, when a golden opportunity arrives with almost zero downside, you swing with everything you have.",
